<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Libu</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 BCE – 700 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Libyan people west of Egypt whose attacks on the Delta alarmed the pharaohs and whose name became the Greek word 'Libya'.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Libu (Rebu) lived along the coast of what is now eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marmarica">Marmarica</a>. They appear in Egyptian records from the 13th century BCE, when, together with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/meshwesh">Meshwesh</a> and seafaring peoples, they tried to move into the fertile western Delta.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of their internal organisation beyond rule by chiefs. Around 1208 BCE their chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meryey">Meryey</a>, son of Did, led a coalition including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah">Merneptah</a>, who defeated it at Perire and claimed over six thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyans">Libyans</a> killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-iii">Ramesses III</a> fought them again. Like the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/meshwesh">Meshwesh</a>, many were later settled in the western Delta, where chiefs bearing the title 'Great Chief of the Libu' ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a> by the 8th century BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Libu chieftaincy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a> gave rise to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/twenty-fourth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-fourth Dynasty of Egypt</a> and later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/twenty-sixth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-sixth Dynasty</a>. Their name passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>', used for all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/libu.jpg" alt="Libu" width="320" height="132"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:From_right_to_left_an_Egyptian,_an_Assyrian,_a_Nubian,_and_Libyans.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 BCE – 700 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Meryey</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Berber (Libyco-Berber)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a>, <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Libyans, Rebu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1000&amp;focus=libu" aria-label="Libu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5161 1207 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Libu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5481 1463h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5481 1463h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Libya. <a href="/atlas?year=-1000&amp;focus=libu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

